// @id 01M6GE7C5BQWTB2MPG0W8EB6MF model=cloze
q: The net number of bonds (the number of bonding pairs minus the number of antibonding pairs) is called ==the Bond Order==. Alternatively, it's the number of chemical bonds (sigma + pi) between a pair of atoms, serving as a formal measure of their covalent bond multiplicity.

// TODO: diagrams

// @id 01M6GE7C5B40HMMYQC9WZZ30W7 model=cloze
q: ==Electronegativity (symbol $\chi$)== is a measure of an atom's tendency to attract shared electrons (or electron density) toward itself when forming a chemical bond.
e: Usually Pauling electronegativity scale is used (0.7-4)

// @id 01M6GE7C5B9DX8RE4B42XJZSEJ model=basic-example
q: What does electronegativity in Pauling scale tell about the bond type?
a: $\Delta_X < 0.4$ - nonpolar covalent (electrons shared evenly)
$0.4 <= \Delta_X < 1.7$ - polar covalent (electrons shared unevenly)
$1.7 <= \Delta_X$ - ionic (electron transfer rather than sharing)

